Reactive Airways Dysfunction Syndrome (RADS)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Abrupt asthma-like onset <24h post-inhalation exposure | Complete absence of prior respiratory disease | Positive methacholine challenge testing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-021
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe shortness of breath and wheezing following chemical exposure, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute respiratory failure or anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset respiratory distress following a high-level irritant exposure. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out evolving bronchospasm, toxic pneumonitis, or acute hypoxic respiratory failure, preventing severe clinical deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I breathed in those cleaning fumes an hour ago, and now my chest is locked tight and I cannot stop coughing.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an abrupt onset of respiratory symptoms following a single, high-level irritant exposure (industrial bleach mixed with ammonia). There is a persistent dry, minimally productive cough accompanied by severe wheezing and a sensation of chest tightness. The patient notes shortness of breath with minimal exertion, despite an absolute absence of prior asthmatic history. There is an exacerbation of symptoms by cold air in the home. The patient denies any fever or purulent pulmonary secretions.
Functional Status: Currently resting in a tripod position, unable to speak in full sentences without coughing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Poor ventilation in the home environment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 24 /min | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious and in moderate respiratory distress, actively wheezing. A general skin examination incidentally notes a symmetrical maculopapular rash with blanching erythematous lesions across the chest, consistent with a concurrent viral prodrome completely distinct from the acute chemical inhalation.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals diffuse bilateral expiratory wheezing and prolonged expiratory phase. Chest wall examination is normal outside of the wheezing, with no paradoxical movement or retractions. Cardiovascular examination is tachycardic but regular. Oropharynx is mildly erythematous but without significant uvular swelling or stridor.
Environmental Observations: A strong odor of chemical cleaning agents is pervasive throughout the residence.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular respiratory medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Reactive Airways Dysfunction Syndrome (RADS) - Status: Acute chemical bronchospasm.
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out clinically due to the absence of hypotension, facial angioedema, stridor, or gastrointestinal symptoms.

Acute Pulmonary Edema - Ruled out due to the absence of bibasilar crackles, normal jugular venous pressure, and lack of cardiac history.

Toxic Pneumonitis - Ruled out for immediate severe risk due to maintenance of oxygen saturations above 92% on room air, though delayed onset remains a risk.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Ongoing chemical exposure in the home).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an immediate after-hours assessment due to acute, severe bronchospasm directly following an intense chemical inhalation. Telephone triage accurately identified the severe risk for rapid airway compromise or toxic pneumonitis. Detailed pulmonary examination confirmed diffuse reactive airway narrowing without acute hypoxemia or anaphylactic shock. By promptly assessing and treating this severe, non-allergic chemical hyperresponsiveness in the home environment, the clinician successfully halted an acute respiratory crisis and averted an unnecessary ambulance dispatch, securing the patient safely while bridging them to outpatient pulmonary testing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered high-dose nebulized bronchodilators and oral corticosteroids. The expected investigation results include a methacholine challenge demonstrating bronchial hyperreactivity with normal resting spirometry and a negative chest radiograph, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via outpatient pulmonary function testing.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote airway clearance and reduce further irritation, strictly isolate yourself in a well-ventilated, chemical-free room with an open window; physically removing yourself from the lingering airborne triggers will immediately calm your hyperreactive airways.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Directed the spouse to thoroughly ventilate the home and dispose of the mixed chemicals safely.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to monitor for delayed toxic pneumonitis.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops blue lips, becomes unable to speak even short words, or if breathing becomes completely silent despite extreme effort.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; chemical airway injuries can exhibit delayed inflammatory phases over the next 24 to 48 hours.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves acute severe bronchospasm followed by a prolonged, hyperresponsive state that can persist for months after the initial exposure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if your inhaler stops providing any relief, or if you feel like you are physically suffocating and cannot catch your breath.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are using strong steroids tonight to forcefully shut down the massive inflammation your lungs have mounted against the chemical fumes, preventing your airways from swelling shut overnight.
Actionable Steps: Sleep completely upright on multiple pillows tonight to maximize your lung expansion and prevent any fluid accumulation in the lower airways.

